{"id": "gsm8k:3699", "benchmark": "gsm8k", "started_unix": 1790771646.549959, "first_event_seconds": 0.9421377169783227, "wall_seconds": 66.35035702795722, "prompt_sha256": "d43548cd2bde8ec32b60c6babd537a7248f1fc333b95f5dadd924f95128fb881", "prompt_tokens": 88, "max_new_tokens": 384, "completion_tokens": 363, "finish_reason": {"type": "stop", "matched": 151645}, "request_id": "5901789f1c1a4324b4a39d11b67428d4", "text": "To determine how many weeks it will take for Carol and Mike to have the same amount of money, we can set up an equation based on their weekly savings.\n\nLet \\( w \\) be the number of weeks.\n\nCarol's total amount of money after \\( w \\) weeks is:\n\\[ 60 + 9w \\]\n\nMike's total amount of money after \\( w \\) weeks is:\n\\[ 50 + 3w \\]\n\nWe need to find the value of \\( w \\) when Carol and Mike have the same amount of money:\n\\[ 60 + 9w = 50 + 3w \\]\n\nFirst, we isolate \\( w \\) by subtracting \\( 3w \\) from both sides:\n\\[ 60 + 6w = 50 \\]\n\nNext, we subtract 60 from both sides:\n\\[ 6w = -10 \\]\n\nThen, we divide both sides by 6:\n\\[ w = -\\frac{10}{6} = -\\frac{5}{3} \\]\n\nSince \\( w \\) must be a non-negative integer (as it represents the number of weeks), and \\(-\\frac{5}{3}\\) is not a valid solution in this context, we need to re-evaluate the problem. The negative value indicates that Carol and Mike will never have the same amount of money if we consider only positive weeks. However, if we consider the problem in a broader context, we can see that Carol will never catch up to Mike because Mike starts with less money but saves more each week.\n\nTherefore, the correct interpretation is that Carol and Mike will never have the same amount of money. \n\nThe answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Never}}\n\\]", "final_answer": null, "gold_answer": "", "gsm_numeric_answer": "3", "gsm_extraction": "last_number", "truncated": false, "events": 363, "output_ids": [1249, 8253, 1246, 1657, 5555, 432, 686, 1896, 369, 10252, 323, 11268, 311, 614,